## Context
original_requirement
# Software Description

## Objective

Develop a web application named 'QuickSearch' that allows users to quickly search for specific words or phrases within a collection of reference materials and books. The application provides an easy way to access relevant information from books.

## Core Features

- Users shall be able to register for an account on the Registration Page by entering a username and password.
- Users shall be able to log in to their account on the Login Page by entering a username and password.
- Users shall be able to search for specific words or phrases within the collection of books by entering them in a search bar on the Dashboard Page.
- Users shall be able to view a list of matching results with relevant book details such as title, author, summary, and cover image.
- Users shall be able to click on a book to view more information, including a detailed description and relevant context on the Book Details Page.
- Users shall be able to add books to their personal reading list by clicking an 'Add to Reading List' button on the Book Details Page.
- Users shall be able to view and manage their reading list on a dedicated Reading List Page.
- Users shall be able to log out of their account, returning to the Login Page.

## Language

Use HTML and Python to develop a web application.

## Data Storage

Data will be stored in local text files.  
Different types of data will be stored in separate `.txt` files.  
This structured data storage will facilitate easy data retrieval and management through simple file manipulations in Python, ensuring the application remains light and efficient without the need for a SQL database.
functional requirements
[OUTPUT]
## general overview of the project
Develop a web application named 'QuickSearch' that allows users to quickly search for specific words or phrases within a collection of reference materials and books. The application provides an easy way to access relevant information from books.
## software functional requirements
1. Users shall be able to register for an account on the Registration Page by entering a username and password.
2. Users shall be able to log in to their account on the Login Page by entering a username and password.
3. Users shall be able to search for specific words or phrases within the collection of books by entering them in a search bar on the Dashboard Page.
4. Users shall be able to view a list of matching results with relevant book details such as title, author, summary, and cover image.
5. Users shall be able to click on a book to view more information, including a detailed description and relevant context on the Book Details Page.
6. Users shall be able to add books to their personal reading list by clicking an 'Add to Reading List' button on the Book Details Page.
7. Users shall be able to view and manage their reading list on a dedicated Reading List Page.
8. Users shall be able to log out of their account, returning to the Login Page.
[/OUTPUT]

## lessons and experience
### Overall Evaluation
- **Strengths:**
  - Flask is well-suited for a lightweight web application, facilitating easy routing, session management, and organization of features.
  - The UI, built with Bootstrap, ensures a clean and responsive experience. The data structures for users, books, and reading lists are clearly organized, aiding in effective manipulation and clarity of responsibilities.

- **Weaknesses:**
  - Reliance on local text files for data storage poses risks for data integrity, retrieval inefficiencies, and potential corruption during concurrent access.
  - The UI lacks key functionalities such as filtering, sorting, and search options for books and reading lists, as well as user feedback mechanisms during registration and login processes.

### Specific Problems and Suggestions
- **User Feedback Deficiencies:**
  - Implement user-friendly error messages for registration and login issues to inform users about problems such as incorrect credentials or registration errors. This could be achieved by enhancing routes and templates with error-handling logic.

- **Insufficient Functionality:**
  - Introduce filtering and sorting options for book searches based on criteria like title, author, or genre. Adding a search bar and controls in the UI will help enhance user engagement. Implement corresponding logic in the `BookManager` and `BookSearch` classes for efficient backend processing.

- **Error Handling for File Operations:**
  - Strengthen error handling during file read/write operations by introducing a logging framework. This can help track errors and unexpected behaviors, providing feedback to users when issues occur.

- **Separation of Concerns:**
  - Refactor the application structure to separate models, routes, and business logic more clearly. Consider using Flask's blueprint feature to organize functionalities like user management and book management into distinct modules.

### Architecture Enhancements
- **Implementation:**
  - While Flask remains appropriate, adopting a modular architecture will better delineate responsibilities and improve maintainability. Use service classes for business logic and clearly define data classes.

- **UI Design Improvements:**
  - Introduce interactive elements such as a search bar, pagination for results, and notifications for user actions (e.g., after adding/removing books). Implement modal dialogs for instant feedback without redirection.

- **Data Structure Optimizations:**
  - Ensure that classes adhere to the single responsibility principle. For instance, methods for managing user sessions and password resets can enhance the `User` class, while directly linking reading lists with users in structured formats will improve usability.

By addressing these areas, the project can significantly enhance its functionality, user experience, and robustness, paving the way for future scalability and efficiency.

